.\" SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-3-Clause
.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt CRON 8
.Os OxideBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm cron
.Nd run scheduled commands
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl nos
.Op Fl j Ar jitter
.Op Fl J Ar rootjitter
.Op Fl m Ar mailto
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
daemon runs commands at the times its tables give, as the users they name.
It is started at boot by
.Pa /etc/rc.d/cron
when
.Va cron_enable
is
.Dq YES
in
.Xr rc.conf 5 .
.Pp
It reads these tables, in the format of
.Xr crontab 5 :
.Bl -bullet
.It
the system table
.Pa /etc/crontab
and every table in
.Pa /etc/cron.d
and
.Pa /usr/local/etc/cron.d ,
whose job lines name the user each job runs as.
Files whose names begin with
.Ql \&.
or
.Ql # ,
or end in
.Ql ~ ,
.Ql \&, ,
.Pa .bak ,
.Pa .orig ,
.Pa .new
or
.Pa .swp ,
are skipped;
.It
each user's table,
.Pa /var/cron/tabs/ Ns Ar user ,
installed with
.Xr crontab 1 .
.El
.Pp
A table that isn't a regular file owned by root and writable only by its owner
is skipped whole, and logged; so is a user's table whose user has no account.
A line that can't be read is logged with its number, and the rest of its table
is used.
.Pp
.Nm
wakes at the start of every minute, or of every second while any table has an
.Cm @every_second
job.
Each time it wakes it reads every table again if any of them, or any of the
directories holding them, has changed, then starts the jobs due.
Times are local time.
.Ss Running a job
Each job runs in a process of its own, so a long job never delays another.
That process waits for the jitter, if any; checks the user's account with the
PAM service
.Dq cron
.Pq Pa /etc/pam.d/cron ,
skipping the job, and logging why, if the account may not run it now; sets
the resource limits, priority and umask of the user's login class
.Pq Xr login.conf 5 ,
the user's groups and user ID; and runs the command with
.Ql $SHELL -c
in the user's home directory.
Text after a
.Ql %
in the command is the job's standard input.
.Pp
A job's environment is
.Ev SHELL Ns = Ns Pa /bin/sh ,
.Ev PATH Ns = Ns Pa /usr/bin:/bin ,
.Ev HOME
from the password file,
.Ev LOGNAME
and
.Ev USER ;
then the environment of the login class; then the table's own settings, which
may replace all of these but
.Ev LOGNAME
and
.Ev USER .
.Pp
Each start is logged at
.Dq cron.info
as
.Ql ( Ns Ar user Ns ) CMD ( Ns Ar command Ns ) .
What the job writes to its standard output and standard error is logged, one
message per line, at
.Dq cron.notice
as
.Ql ( Ns Ar user Ns ) CMDOUT ( Ns Ar line Ns ) ,
unless
.Ev MAILTO
is set to the empty string.
.Ss Special times
An
.Cm @reboot
job runs when
.Nm
starts, once per boot:
.Nm
then creates
.Pa /var/run/cron.reboot ,
which
.Pa /etc/rc.d/cleanvar
removes at the next boot.
.Pp
When the clock moves forward by up to three hours, a job with a fixed time
.Pq neither its minute nor its hour is Ql *
that fell in the skipped time runs once; other jobs run only for the new time.
When it moves back by up to three hours, only the jobs whose minute or hour is
.Ql *
run until the clock is past where it was, so that no fixed-time job runs twice.
A larger change starts again from the new time.
.Pp
The options are:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl j Ar jitter
Delay each job of a user other than root by a random number of seconds, from 0
to
.Ar jitter
(at most 60).
.It Fl J Ar rootjitter
The same for root's jobs.
.It Fl m Ar mailto
The value of
.Ev MAILTO
for tables that don't set it.
.It Fl n
Stay in the foreground.
.It Fl s
Treat a change between standard and daylight-saving time as a change of the
clock: a fixed-time job whose time is skipped runs once, and one whose time is
repeated doesn't run again.
.It Fl o
Don't: a skipped time is skipped, and a repeated one runs its jobs again.
This is the default.
If both
.Fl s
and
.Fl o
are given, the last one counts.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width /var/run/cron.reboot -compact
.It Pa /etc/crontab
the system table
.It Pa /etc/cron.d
more system tables
.It Pa /var/cron/tabs
the users' tables
.It Pa /var/run/cron.pid
the process ID, locked while
.Nm
runs, so that a second one exits
.It Pa /var/run/cron.reboot
present once the
.Cm @reboot
jobs have run
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr crontab 1 ,
.Xr crontab 5 ,
.Xr login.conf 5 ,
.Xr syslog.conf 5 ,
.Xr periodic 8
.Sh HISTORY
A
.Nm
command appeared in
.At v7 .
OxideBSD's follows Paul Vixie's, which the BSDs share, with
.Fx Ns 's
options.
.Sh BUGS
There is no mail system, so a job's output is logged instead of mailed, and
.Fl m
and
.Ev MAILTO
only decide whether it is kept.
